    "rates": "https://www.soa.org/resources/research-reports/2026/us-historical-mortality-rates/ (Society of Actuaries, U.S. Population Mortality Rates 2000-2024, October 2026); data file https://www.soa.org/globalassets/assets/files/resources/research-report/2026/us-historical-mortality-rates/2000-24-us-pop-mort-rates.xlsx",
    "underlying_data": "Same data sets as the SSA historical probabilities of death: CDC WONDER death counts and Census Bureau population estimates (per the SOA publication).",
    "cross_check": "SOA RPEC Scale MP-2021 remains the latest retirement-plan improvement scale as of the October 2026 RPEC update: https://www.soa.org/resources/research-reports/2026/rpec-mortality-improvement-update/"
  },
  "method_notes": {
    "base_table": "2024 one-year death probabilities by sex, single ages 20-100. These are crude population rates (all US residents), not annuitant or insured tables.",
    "extrapolation": "Ages 101-120: Gompertz fit to ages 90-100, capped at 1, non-decreasing, q(120) = 1.",
    "improvement": "Each age's trend is a log-linear fit over 2000-2019, smoothed over +/-5 ages, clipped to 0%-1.5% a year, fading to zero from age 100 to 110; young-adult trends are negative (drug deaths) and clipped to 0. In use, the trend fades linearly from the base year to the SOA Scale MP-2021 long-term rate for the age (1.35% to age 62, 1.10% at 80, 0.40% at 95, 0% at 115; read from the MP-2021 report, see improvement_ultimate_source) by 2037, when MP-2021 reaches its ultimate rates, and then stays there (never above the age's own trend). The age pattern of the trend is our estimate, not the SOA's scale.",
    "rounding": "Source rates are published to 5 decimals, which limits precision at young ages."
  },
